We need to design a PLC program for mechanical arm control. The interface is defined. We must implement a FUNCTION_BLOCK in CODESYS ST. The task is from a benchmark; we need to output complete ST code. We'll need to manage modes, actions, start button edge detection (single step, single cycle, continuous). Emergency stop overrides everything. Manual mode is straightforward: if not at limit and button pressed, output movement. Note: manual mode: "provided it is not yet in position" and "releasing button stops movement". So outputs high only when button pressed and limit switch false. Could also require that emergency stop is not active? The requirement #5 says emergency stop stops all actions at any time. So in all modes, if EmergencyStop = FALSE (triggered, because they define TRUE: no emergency stop triggered, FALSE: triggered), then outputs must be false. So we need to check EmergencyStop first; if false, disable all outputs. In manual mode, buttons directly drive outputs, but if emergency stop, off.

Single step mode: each start button press advances one step. The sequence is: down to down limit, grip, up, right, down, release, up, left, then cycle repeats. Initially at top left. Need to manage step index and detect rising edge of Start. The start button pressed once to execute one action, and after each action completed, wait for next press. So we need state machine with steps. Need to trigger action for each step, and when limit reached, action completes. For grip/release, completion is limit switch. So output for action should be active until its completion limit switch becomes true, then turn off and wait for next start press. But note: in single step, the operator confirms after each action is completed to execute next action. That means after an action completes, the system stops and waits for next start press. So outputs should remain off after completion until next start press. Also need to handle start press: when start pressed and action not active? It should start the next action. We need to avoid multiple actions starting if start is held; use edge detection. After action completes, the state machine should be in "waiting for start" state. When start pressed, advance step and turn on output for that step. Then the action runs until its completion condition is true; then output off, go to waiting for start for next step. Note: for step 9 (left move), after completion, cycle repeats from step 1 but still waiting for start.

Single cycle mode: when start pressed, auto-run full cycle: down, grip, up, right, down, release, up, left, stop. It should complete all actions without further start presses, then stop at end. So we need a mode where after start pressed, it proceeds through actions automatically, each action completes based on limit switch, then next action immediately starts, until all done, then stop. Should it need to wait at each step? No, it says automatically completes full cycle. So output should transition based on limit switch completions.

Continuous mode: start pressed, repeatedly executes cycle continuously. So after completing last action (left), it should immediately start again from beginning without waiting.

Emergency stop: stops all actions. If emergency stop triggers, what happens to state? Should pause/reset? The requirement only says "stop all actions at any time". It doesn't specify state preservation. Usually emergency stop would halt and require manual reset, but not specified. We can simply set all outputs false and perhaps freeze state. In single step, after emergency stop is cleared, the system should resume? Not specified. Since it's a PLC program for benchmark, likely they expect outputs zero when emergency stop is active, and maybe the automation continues from where it left off when released. However, typical safety would require manual reset, but not mentioned. We need to make a decision. The interface has EmergencyStop as input, TRUE means no stop, FALSE means stop triggered. So our logic: if EmergencyStop = FALSE, all outputs = FALSE, and also we probably should not allow any mode actions to start/continue. But state machine could be held in current state? If we keep state unchanged, when emergency stop released, outputs would resume according to current state. That might be acceptable, but might cause unexpected motion. For simplicity, we can freeze all outputs and state while emergency stop is active, by suppressing the entire logic (like if EmergencyStop then outputs all false and don't update state). But we need to ensure that output assignments occur regardless of condition, but we can conditionally override. We can structure code: first compute outputs based on mode logic without considering emergency stop, then at end if not EmergencyStop then set all outputs false. This is straightforward.

But careful: in manual mode, emergency stop should disable outputs even if buttons pressed.

Now for mode selection: the four mode inputs are mutually exclusive, so only one is true at a time. We can use IF/ELSIF.

Need to handle start button edge detection. We'll need rising edge detection for Start in single step and single cycle/continuous. In single step, start triggers next action only when the system is in "waiting" state (after previous action completion and before next action). In single cycle/continuous, start triggers cycle start. After cycle starts, subsequent start presses should be ignored? Probably yes, they should start only when stopped at initial position or after cycle complete (single cycle) or continuously (continuous). For continuous, start would start the continuous cycle, and if pressed again while running, maybe ignored. So we need a "running" flag for single cycle and continuous. In single cycle, after cycle completes, running = false and waits for start. In continuous, running stays true after start, so continuous operation. If emergency stop occurs, maybe should set running = false? But not specified; we can keep as is and when emergency stop released, it might resume if running was true. But safer to maybe set running = false on emergency stop, requiring start again. That is typical. Let's do that: when emergency stop is triggered, set running = false and also reset state? But we don't want to overcomplicate. Let's design: in non-manual modes, we have an internal state machine. We'll use a step index (1..8, or 1..9? The sequence is: down, grip, up, right, down, release, up, left: that's 8 actions? Wait, they said: "first moves down, then grips after reaching the down position, moves up after gripping, moves right after reaching the up position, moves down after reaching the right position, releases after reaching the down position, moves up after releasing, moves left after reaching the up position, and stops after reaching the left position." That is 8 actions: 1) down, 2) grip, 3) up, 4) right, 5) down, 6) release, 7) up, 8) left. After left, stop. So 8 steps. For single step, they listed: down, grip, up, right, down, release, up, left, then cycle repeats. So 8 steps as well, then after left completion, go back to step1 but waiting. So step index 1..8. For continuous, after step8 completes, go back to step1 and continue automatically.

Now design state variables:

- Step: INT (1..8) representing current action in automated sequence.
- ActionInProgress: BOOL, true when an action command is active (outputs high). It is set when an action is started (after start press or after previous action completes in auto mode), and cleared when the action's completion condition becomes true (limit switch reached) or when emergency stop.
- For single step: we need to differentiate between waiting for start and action in progress. Could use Step and a flag like WaitingForStart. When action completes, set WaitingForStart = TRUE, output off. When start rising edge detected and WaitingForStart, start next step: WaitingForStart = FALSE, ActionInProgress = TRUE, set output based on step. At step8 completion, after left limit reached, set WaitingForStart = TRUE and reset Step to 1 (or keep as 8? Then next start goes to step1). Better to reset to 1 after completion, so next start starts step1.

- For single cycle: after start rising edge, set CycleRunning = TRUE, Step = 1, ActionInProgress = TRUE. Upon action completion, if step < 8, increment step and start next action, still CycleRunning. If step = 8 completion, set CycleRunning = FALSE, ActionInProgress = FALSE, and wait for next start.

- For continuous: similar to single cycle but after step8 completion, do not set CycleRunning = FALSE; instead set Step = 1 and continue. So CycleRunning remains TRUE indefinitely.

- Emergency stop: when EmergencyStop = FALSE, we set outputs false, and we also should probably set ActionInProgress = FALSE, CycleRunning = FALSE, WaitingForStart = TRUE? But we need to define behavior after release. Perhaps we can force the state machine to an idle state: in single step, waiting for start with Step = 1? In single cycle/continuous, CycleRunning = FALSE, waiting for start. That is safer and simpler. So on emergency stop (detected as EmergencyStop = FALSE), set all outputs false, and reset state: Step = 1, ActionInProgress = FALSE, CycleRunning = FALSE, WaitingForStart = TRUE (for single step). But this would discard progress; user would have to start over from initial position? Not necessarily, but it's acceptable. However, if the arm is not at initial position, starting over might cause issues because limits might already be triggered. But emergency stop likely requires manual repositioning; it's beyond scope. We'll just reset to idle state, assuming the operator will manually move to initial if needed. This is common in simple benchmark tasks.

But we need to handle Start edge detection also when emergency stop is active? Probably ignore start if emergency stop active. We'll simply not process start edges when not EmergencyStop.

Now manual mode: independent of state machine. Outputs directly from buttons and limit switches. Need to ensure no conflicting outputs: if multiple buttons pressed? Probably mutually exclusive? Not guaranteed, but we can allow multiple outputs if multiple buttons pressed? Usually a selector ensures only one axis at a time. We'll assume buttons are exclusive, but we can design as if each axis independently controlled by its button and limit. So MoveDown = DownButton AND NOT DownLimitSwitch; similarly for others. Grip/Release similar. However, note that manual mode should be "provided it is not yet in position", meaning if at limit, don't move further. So output = button AND NOT limit. That's fine.

Now automated modes: we need to map step to output command. Steps:
1: MoveDown until DownLimitSwitch
2: ActivateGrip until GripLimitSwitch
3: MoveUp until UpLimitSwitch
4: MoveRight until RightLimitSwitch
5: MoveDown (again) until DownLimitSwitch
6: ActivateRelease until ReleaseLimitSwitch
7: MoveUp until UpLimitSwitch
8: MoveLeft until LeftLimitSwitch

So each step has an output and completion condition.

We'll implement a CASE statement for step to set output and monitor completion.

We need to be careful about output assignments: in automated modes, only one action active at a time, so outputs for that action = TRUE, others false.

We'll need to store internal variables. We'll use a helper block or internal variables. Since we are writing a FUNCTION_BLOCK, we can declare VARs. We need to maintain state across scans.

We need to detect rising edge of Start: use a variable StartPrev to store previous state of Start, and on transition from FALSE to TRUE, set a flag StartPulse for one scan. But careful: if Start input is boolean and we store in variable, we can do:

IF Start AND NOT StartPrev THEN StartPulse := TRUE; ELSE StartPulse := FALSE; END_IF; StartPrev := Start;

However, we need to avoid multiple pulses if Start is held high across scans. That works.

Now for the three automated modes, we can have a shared state variable, but we need to differentiate behavior. We can have separate logic branches.

Let's define internal variables:

VAR
    Step: INT := 1; // 1..8
    ActionActive: BOOL := FALSE; // true when an automated action command is being output
    WaitingForStart: BOOL := TRUE; // for single step: true when waiting for next start press
    CycleRunning: BOOL := FALSE; // for single cycle and continuous: true when a cycle is in progress
    StartPrev: BOOL := FALSE;
    StartPulse: BOOL := FALSE;
    // For manual, no internal state needed.
END_VAR

We'll also need to compute outputs. We'll initialize outputs to false then set accordingly. We'll structure:

IF NOT EmergencyStop THEN
   // Emergency stop active: all outputs false, reset state.
   MoveDown := FALSE; MoveUp := FALSE; MoveLeft := FALSE; MoveRight := FALSE; ActivateGrip := FALSE; ActivateRelease := FALSE;
   Step := 1;
   ActionActive := FALSE;
   WaitingForStart := TRUE;
   CycleRunning := FALSE;
   // also reset StartPrev? Could keep, but when emergency stop released, edge detection might be off. We can set StartPrev := Start to prevent false pulse. But StartPrev is internal, we can handle later.
ELSE
   // Normal operation
   IF ManualMode THEN
       // Manual mode: direct control
       MoveDown := DownButton AND NOT DownLimitSwitch;
       MoveUp := UpButton AND NOT UpLimitSwitch;
       MoveLeft := LeftButton AND NOT LeftLimitSwitch;
       MoveRight := RightButton AND NOT RightLimitSwitch;
       ActivateGrip := GripButton AND NOT GripLimitSwitch;
       ActivateRelease := ReleaseButton AND NOT ReleaseLimitSwitch;
       // Reset automated state? Not necessary, but to avoid stale, set Step:=1, etc.
       Step := 1;
       ActionActive := FALSE;
       WaitingForStart := TRUE;
       CycleRunning := FALSE;
   ELSIF SingleStepMode THEN
       // Single step mode
       // Edge detection for start
       // We compute StartPulse before branching? We can compute globally at top of if EmergencyStop.
       // But StartPulse is computed once per scan, independent of mode. Place before mode logic.
       // We'll place edge detection before the IF mode selection.

       // Single step logic: if waiting for start and start pulse, start next step.
       IF WaitingForStart AND StartPulse THEN
           WaitingForStart := FALSE;
           ActionActive := TRUE;
           Step := 1; // always start from step1? But after cycle completes, we reset to 1. So yes, start at step1.
           // Output will be set below based on step.
       END_IF;
       // If action active, monitor completion.
       IF ActionActive THEN
           // Check completion for current step.
           CASE Step OF
               1: IF DownLimitSwitch THEN ActionActive := FALSE; WaitingForStart := TRUE; Step := 1; END_IF;
               2: IF GripLimitSwitch THEN ActionActive := FALSE; WaitingForStart := TRUE; Step := 2; END_IF;
               3: IF UpLimitSwitch THEN ActionActive := FALSE; WaitingForStart := TRUE; Step := 3; END_IF;
               4: IF RightLimitSwitch THEN ActionActive := FALSE; WaitingForStart := TRUE; Step := 4; END_IF;
               5: IF DownLimitSwitch THEN ActionActive := FALSE; WaitingForStart := TRUE; Step := 5; END_IF;
               6: IF ReleaseLimitSwitch THEN ActionActive := FALSE; WaitingForStart := TRUE; Step := 6; END_IF;
               7: IF UpLimitSwitch THEN ActionActive := FALSE; WaitingForStart := TRUE; Step := 7; END_IF;
               8: IF LeftLimitSwitch THEN ActionActive := FALSE; WaitingForStart := TRUE; Step := 1; END_IF;
           END_CASE;
           // After action completion, outputs will be set false because ActionActive is false, so we can set outputs based on ActionActive and Step.
       END_IF;
       // Set outputs based on Step if ActionActive.
       MoveDown := FALSE; MoveUp := FALSE; MoveLeft := FALSE; MoveRight := FALSE; ActivateGrip := FALSE; ActivateRelease := FALSE;
       IF ActionActive THEN
           CASE Step OF
               1: MoveDown := TRUE;
               2: ActivateGrip := TRUE;
               3: MoveUp := TRUE;
               4: MoveRight := TRUE;
               5: MoveDown := TRUE;
               6: ActivateRelease := TRUE;
               7: MoveUp := TRUE;
               8: MoveLeft := TRUE;
           END_CASE;
       END_IF;
   ELSIF SingleCycleMode THEN
       // Single cycle mode
       IF StartPulse AND NOT CycleRunning THEN
           CycleRunning := TRUE;
           Step := 1;
           ActionActive := TRUE;
       END_IF;
       IF CycleRunning THEN
           // Check completion
           IF ActionActive THEN
               CASE Step OF
                   1: IF DownLimitSwitch THEN ActionActive := FALSE; Step := 2; END_IF;
                   2: IF GripLimitSwitch THEN ActionActive := FALSE; Step := 3; END_IF;
                   3: IF UpLimitSwitch THEN ActionActive := FALSE; Step := 4; END_IF;
                   4: IF RightLimitSwitch THEN ActionActive := FALSE; Step := 5; END_IF;
                   5: IF DownLimitSwitch THEN ActionActive := FALSE; Step := 6; END_IF;
                   6: IF ReleaseLimitSwitch THEN ActionActive := FALSE; Step := 7; END_IF;
                   7: IF UpLimitSwitch THEN ActionActive := FALSE; Step := 8; END_IF;
                   8: IF LeftLimitSwitch THEN ActionActive := FALSE; CycleRunning := FALSE; Step := 1; END_IF;
               END_CASE;
               // After transition, if ActionActive became false and CycleRunning still true, we need to start next action immediately.
               // So handle transition: if NOT ActionActive AND CycleRunning AND Step >= 2 AND Step <= 8? Actually after Step change to next, we need to set ActionActive = TRUE for new step.
               // But we set ActionActive false, then we need to set it true again for the next step. We can do in same block.
               // However, we need to distinguish between action completion that ends cycle (step8) and those that continue.
               // For step1-7, after completion and increment step, we should set ActionActive := TRUE.
               // We'll handle by checking if CycleRunning AND NOT ActionActive AND Step <> 1? Wait, after step8 completion we set CycleRunning false, so no start. For other steps, we set ActionActive false then immediately set true for next step.
           END_IF;
           // Actually better: after detecting completion and incrementing step, we set ActionActive = TRUE for new step (unless cycle ended). So internal code needs careful ordering.
           // We'll restructure: When ActionActive and completion condition met, do:
           //   IF Step < 8 THEN
           //       Step := Step + 1;
           //       // ActionActive remains TRUE? Actually we want next action to start immediately, so we can keep ActionActive = TRUE and just change Step. We don't need to turn off output in between because it's instantaneous. So we can simply change Step, and the output mapping will change immediately. There is no need to set ActionActive FALSE then TRUE. So we can do: if completion, then increment step, and continue. For step8, completion ends cycle, set ActionActive FALSE, CycleRunning FALSE.
           // That is simpler.
       END_IF;
       // Set outputs based on Step and ActionActive/CycleRunning.
       MoveDown := FALSE; ...;
       IF CycleRunning AND ActionActive THEN
           CASE Step OF ...
           END_CASE;
       END_IF;
   ELSIF ContinuousMode THEN
       // Continuous mode
       IF StartPulse AND NOT CycleRunning THEN
           CycleRunning := TRUE;
           Step := 1;
           ActionActive := TRUE;
       END_IF;
       IF CycleRunning THEN
           IF ActionActive THEN
               CASE Step OF
                   1: IF DownLimitSwitch THEN Step := 2; END_IF;
                   2: IF GripLimitSwitch THEN Step := 3; END_IF;
                   3: IF UpLimitSwitch THEN Step := 4; END_IF;
                   4: IF RightLimitSwitch THEN Step := 5; END_IF;
                   5: IF DownLimitSwitch THEN Step := 6; END_IF;
                   6: IF ReleaseLimitSwitch THEN Step := 7; END_IF;
                   7: IF UpLimitSwitch THEN Step := 8; END_IF;
                   8: IF LeftLimitSwitch THEN Step := 1; END_IF; // cycle continues, stay running
               END_CASE;
               // ActionActive remains TRUE throughout, no need to change.
           END_IF;
       END_IF;
       // Set outputs
       MoveDown := FALSE; ...;
       IF CycleRunning AND ActionActive THEN
           CASE Step OF ...
           END_CASE;
       END_IF;
   END_IF;
END_IF;

But we must also compute StartPulse globally before mode branches, but only when EmergencyStop is TRUE? Actually StartPulse should be computed regardless, but if emergency stop is active, we might not want to act on it. We can compute StartPulse every scan based on Start and StartPrev. Then in mode logic, we only act on StartPulse if EmergencyStop is TRUE (i.e., not emergency stop). Since we are inside the ELSE branch (EmergencyStop TRUE), it's fine. So we compute StartPulse at the top of the FUNCTION_BLOCK, before all logic? But if we put it before the EmergencyStop check, then we update StartPrev even when emergency stop. That's okay. But we need to be careful: after emergency stop, we might want to reset StartPrev to avoid false pulses. We can set StartPrev := Start in the emergency stop branch to synchronize, so no false pulse when released. But that's optional.

We'll code:

VAR
    Step: INT := 1;
    ActionActive: BOOL := FALSE;
    WaitingForStart: BOOL := TRUE;
    CycleRunning: BOOL := FALSE;
    StartPrev: BOOL := FALSE;
    StartPulse: BOOL := FALSE;
END_VAR

Then in body:

// Rising edge detection for Start
StartPulse := Start AND NOT StartPrev;
StartPrev := Start;

IF NOT EmergencyStop THEN
    // Emergency stop triggered: all outputs off, reset state
    MoveDown := FALSE; MoveUp := FALSE; MoveLeft := FALSE; MoveRight := FALSE; ActivateGrip := FALSE; ActivateRelease := FALSE;
    Step := 1;
    ActionActive := FALSE;
    WaitingForStart := TRUE;
    CycleRunning := FALSE;
ELSE
    // Mode logic
    IF ManualMode THEN
        MoveDown := DownButton AND NOT DownLimitSwitch;
        MoveUp := UpButton AND NOT UpLimitSwitch;
        MoveLeft := LeftButton AND NOT LeftLimitSwitch;
        MoveRight := RightButton AND NOT RightLimitSwitch;
        ActivateGrip := GripButton AND NOT GripLimitSwitch;
        ActivateRelease := ReleaseButton AND NOT ReleaseLimitSwitch;
        // Reset automated state
        Step := 1;
        ActionActive := FALSE;
        WaitingForStart := TRUE;
        CycleRunning := FALSE;
    ELSIF SingleStepMode THEN
        // Default outputs false
        MoveDown := FALSE; MoveUp := FALSE; MoveLeft := FALSE; MoveRight := FALSE; ActivateGrip := FALSE; ActivateRelease := FALSE;
        IF WaitingForStart AND StartPulse THEN
            WaitingForStart := FALSE;
            ActionActive := TRUE;
            Step := 1; // start from step 1
        END_IF;
        IF ActionActive THEN
            // Check completion
            CASE Step OF
                1: IF DownLimitSwitch THEN ActionActive := FALSE; WaitingForStart := TRUE; Step := 1; END_IF;
                2: IF GripLimitSwitch THEN ActionActive := FALSE; WaitingForStart := TRUE; Step := 2; END_IF;
                3: IF UpLimitSwitch THEN ActionActive := FALSE; WaitingForStart := TRUE; Step := 3; END_IF;
                4: IF RightLimitSwitch THEN ActionActive := FALSE; WaitingForStart := TRUE; Step := 4; END_IF;
                5: IF DownLimitSwitch THEN ActionActive := FALSE; WaitingForStart := TRUE; Step := 5; END_IF;
                6: IF ReleaseLimitSwitch THEN ActionActive := FALSE; WaitingForStart := TRUE; Step := 6; END_IF;
                7: IF UpLimitSwitch THEN ActionActive := FALSE; WaitingForStart := TRUE; Step := 7; END_IF;
                8: IF LeftLimitSwitch THEN ActionActive := FALSE; WaitingForStart := TRUE; Step := 1; END_IF;
            END_CASE;
        END_IF;
        IF ActionActive THEN
            CASE Step OF
                1: MoveDown := TRUE;
                2: ActivateGrip := TRUE;
                3: MoveUp := TRUE;
                4: MoveRight := TRUE;
                5: MoveDown := TRUE;
                6: ActivateRelease := TRUE;
                7: MoveUp := TRUE;
                8: MoveLeft := TRUE;
            END_CASE;
        END_IF;
    ELSIF SingleCycleMode THEN
        MoveDown := FALSE; MoveUp := FALSE; MoveLeft := FALSE; MoveRight := FALSE; ActivateGrip := FALSE; ActivateRelease := FALSE;
        IF NOT CycleRunning AND StartPulse THEN
            CycleRunning := TRUE;
            Step := 1;
            ActionActive := TRUE;
        END_IF;
        IF CycleRunning AND ActionActive THEN
            CASE Step OF
                1: IF DownLimitSwitch THEN Step := 2; END_IF;
                2: IF GripLimitSwitch THEN Step := 3; END_IF;
                3: IF UpLimitSwitch THEN Step := 4; END_IF;
                4: IF RightLimitSwitch THEN Step := 5; END_IF;
                5: IF DownLimitSwitch THEN Step := 6; END_IF;
                6: IF ReleaseLimitSwitch THEN Step := 7; END_IF;
                7: IF UpLimitSwitch THEN Step := 8; END_IF;
                8: IF LeftLimitSwitch THEN
                      ActionActive := FALSE;
                      CycleRunning := FALSE;
                      Step := 1; // reset for next cycle
                   END_IF;
            END_CASE;
        END_IF;
        IF CycleRunning AND ActionActive THEN
            CASE Step OF
                1: MoveDown := TRUE;
                2: ActivateGrip := TRUE;
                3: MoveUp := TRUE;
                4: MoveRight := TRUE;
                5: MoveDown := TRUE;
                6: ActivateRelease := TRUE;
                7: MoveUp := TRUE;
                8: MoveLeft := TRUE;
            END_CASE;
        END_IF;
    ELSIF ContinuousMode THEN
        MoveDown := FALSE; MoveUp := FALSE; MoveLeft := FALSE; MoveRight := FALSE; ActivateGrip := FALSE; ActivateRelease := FALSE;
        IF NOT CycleRunning AND StartPulse THEN
            CycleRunning := TRUE;
            Step := 1;
            ActionActive := TRUE;
        END_IF;
        IF CycleRunning AND ActionActive THEN
            CASE Step OF
                1: IF DownLimitSwitch THEN Step := 2; END_IF;
                2: IF GripLimitSwitch THEN Step := 3; END_IF;
                3: IF UpLimitSwitch THEN Step := 4; END_IF;
                4: IF RightLimitSwitch THEN Step := 5; END_IF;
                5: IF DownLimitSwitch THEN Step := 6; END_IF;
                6: IF ReleaseLimitSwitch THEN Step := 7; END_IF;
                7: IF UpLimitSwitch THEN Step := 8; END_IF;
                8: IF LeftLimitSwitch THEN Step := 1; END_IF; // loop
            END_CASE;
        END_IF;
        IF CycleRunning AND ActionActive THEN
            CASE Step OF
                1: MoveDown := TRUE;
                2: ActivateGrip := TRUE;
                3: MoveUp := TRUE;
                4: MoveRight := TRUE;
                5: MoveDown := TRUE;
                6: ActivateRelease := TRUE;
                7: MoveUp := TRUE;
                8: MoveLeft := TRUE;
            END_CASE;
        END_IF;
    END_IF;
END_IF;

Now, in continuous mode, after step8 completion, step becomes 1, and since ActionActive remains TRUE (we didn't change it), it will continue with step1. That is correct. However, we should ensure that ActionActive remains TRUE; it is set to TRUE at start, and never set to FALSE except emergency stop or mode change. So continuous runs forever until emergency stop or mode switch.

In single cycle, after step8 completion, ActionActive set false, CycleRunning false, step reset to1, so it stops.

In single step, after action completion, ActionActive false, WaitingForStart true, step left at completed step or reset depending. We reset step to same step number except step8 resets to1. This is fine; next start will always set step=1, so step value doesn't matter. But we keep step as it was for clarity.

Potential issue: In single step, when waiting and start pressed, we set step=1 always. That means after completing step8, the next start will again do step1, as desired. But what if the sequence is not at initial position? It assumes initial each time. That's acceptable per requirements.

Now, we must ensure that outputs are set only in the mode branch. In manual mode, we set outputs directly. In automated modes, we initialize outputs false and then set if action active. In emergency stop, we set all false. Also when no mode is selected (should not happen, but if all false), we should default outputs false. The ELSE branch of the main IF EmergencyStop is only executed when EmergencyStop is TRUE. Within that, we have IF ManualMode ... ELSIF ... but what if no mode selected? We should add an ELSE to set all outputs false and reset. We can add an ELSE after the mode ELSIF chain to ensure outputs are false when no mode. But the mode inputs are mutually exclusive, so at least one is true. Nevertheless, best to include ELSE. We'll add:

ELSE
    MoveDown := FALSE; MoveUp := FALSE; MoveLeft := FALSE; MoveRight := FALSE; ActivateGrip := FALSE; ActivateRelease := FALSE;
    Step := 1; ActionActive := FALSE; WaitingForStart := TRUE; CycleRunning := FALSE;
END_IF;

That is inside the EmergencyStop TRUE branch, after the mode ELSIF chain. Actually, we need to structure: after all ELSIF, we can have an ELSE that catches any case. Or we can rely on initialization. We'll include it.

Now we need to be careful with variable declarations: BOOL outputs must be assigned in all paths. In the emergency stop branch, we assign all outputs. In the normal branch, each mode branch assigns outputs. Also the ELSE for no mode assigns outputs. So it's fine.

Now about StartPulse: We compute it every scan regardless of emergency stop. But in single cycle/continuous, we check StartPulse AND NOT CycleRunning. If Start is held, StartPulse will be true for one scan only. Good.

Potential race: In single step, after action completes (ActionActive set false, WaitingForStart set true) in same scan as limit switch becomes true, if start is also pressed in same scan, it could start next action immediately because the IF WaitingForStart AND StartPulse is before the completion check. But we have ordering: we first check start pulse, then check completion. So if both happen same scan (unlikely but possible), the start pulse will be processed first, setting ActionActive true and step=1, then the completion check will see ActionActive true and might immediately see limit switch still true and complete the new action erroneously. For example, after step1 (down) completes, DownLimitSwitch becomes true, and simultaneously user presses start. In same scan, StartPulse triggers start of step1 again (since waiting was true) but DownLimitSwitch is still true, so action completes immediately. That would cause a step to be skipped. To avoid this, we should process completion before start pulse? Or add a flag to avoid starting if just completed? Since start pulse is edge, it's only one scan, and completion condition is level. Typically limit switch would be true for longer than one scan? But could be just at transition. To be safe, we should order: check completion first, then check start pulse. But if we check completion first, when action active and limit reached, we set ActionActive false, WaitingForStart true, Step updated. Then later in same scan, we check start pulse and if present, we start again. That still has same issue if start pulse is present same scan as completion. So we need to ensure that after completion, we ignore start pulse in that scan. We can add a flag like JustCompleted to inhibit start in same scan. Or we can reorder: start pulse check only if NOT ActionActive AND WaitingForStart? But after completion, ActionActive is false and WaitingForStart true, so it would still trigger. Better: use a CompletionPulse flag, but simple solution: we can move the start pulse check after the completion check, and also add condition that it only starts if no completion just happened. That is complex. Simpler: we can make the start pulse detection occur only at the beginning of the scan before any state changes, and then use that pulse only if the state was already waiting at the beginning of the scan (before any completion). That would require storing a previous WaitingForStart state. We can store WaitingForStartPrev at the beginning before any logic, and then use StartPulse AND WaitingForStartPrev. That ensures that if waiting was true at scan start (meaning no completion in prior scan), then a start pulse can start. If completion occurs in the same scan, WaitingForStartPrev would still be false if it was previously false (because it was action active), so start pulse would be ignored. So we can implement:

At the start of the normal logic (before mode branches), we can capture the state of WaitingForStart into a local variable, or we can use the current WaitingForStart but ensure that completion and start check are ordered such that start check uses the state from before completion. We'll add a variable WaitingForStartPrev: BOOL, and set it at the beginning of single step mode processing? Actually we can set it at the very beginning of the function block, after edge detection, before mode logic, to capture the state from previous scan. Then within single step, we use that for the condition.

